WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.580
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.400
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:24.530
Hello.

9
00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:32.200
I just want to say, I am so happy to be sitting across this recording booth table.

10
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:33.240
Is it a table?

11
00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:33.860
It's a table.

12
00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:34.600
Yeah.

13
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:36.260
From my best friend this morning.

14
00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:37.460
Thanks, lady.

15
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:38.120
Yeah.

16
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.780
I am super peppy this morning.

17
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:42.020
That's awesome.

18
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:47.220
I'm really peppy and I got no sleep, which means I'm more loopy than peppy.

19
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:48.500
So buckle in.

20
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.700
What is in store for us today?

21
00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:51.740
I don't know.

22
00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.520
Would you like an update on my contrast showers before we begin today?

23
00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:58.000
Yeah.

24
00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.840
I've taken them to a new level.

25
00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:01.540
I don't shower anymore.

26
00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:02.560
I'm sorry, what?

27
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.840
Well, I mean, I shower for cleanliness, obviously.

28
00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:19.740
Obviously, my way of doing the contrast hydrotherapy shower is now I sit in the hot tub and it's super hot and then I do a cold dip in our freezing cold swimming pool.

29
00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.140
Why, Chumma?

30
00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:22.520
It is so cold.

31
00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.080
I am on fire about it.

32
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:25.760
You know what?

33
00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.240
This is unlocking something in me.

34
00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:31.480
I am starting to read books about it.

35
00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:37.280
Wait, does this mean Office Ladies is going to go somewhere really icy and do a polar bear plunge?

36
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:38.520
Are we going to Finland?

37
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:44.060
Are we going to have a shot of some kind of alcohol and then jump in an icy lake?

38
00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.040
I don't know if I'm at icy lake yet.

39
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.920
I mean, I'm at pretty cold.

40
00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:49.040
Would you do it, though?

41
00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:49.460
No.

42
00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:50.700
Here's the thing.

43
00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.200
Lee is really into it.

44
00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:56.460
Lee wants to talk to Josh about it because Josh, you know, is a super healthy guy.

45
00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:56.800
Yeah.

46
00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.600
I think he's going to get Josh to start doing it.

47
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:03.000
Lee and I think maybe 2022 we might get you to do it, Ange.

48
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:03.560
No way.

49
00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:04.600
I hate being cold.

50
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.660
I want it to be something that you do.

51
00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:07.360
No!

52
00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:09.580
I don't know why.

53
00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.040
I don't want to do it.

54
00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:12.680
All right, fine.

55
00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.440
I would do it on a vacation.

56
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.200
I am vacation adventurous.

57
00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:18.860
I am not day-to-day adventurous.

58
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:19.520
All right.

59
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.000
Take me, you know, somewhere Nordic.

60
00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:23.120
All right.

61
00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:24.280
Maybe I'll jump in.

62
00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:24.960
All right.

63
00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:27.240
And holler after I've had a shot of something.

64
00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:28.360
Okay.

65
00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.000
That's probably not the health routine you want.

66
00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:38.340
Well, today we are going to talk about The Meeting, which is Season 6, Episode 2.

67
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.320
It is written by one of our favorite people, Erin Schur.

68
00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:43.420
Love him.

69
00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:47.360
And directed by one of our other favorite people, Randall Einhorn.

70
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:47.900
Yeah.

71
00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:49.260
Here's a summary.

72
00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.180
Jim meets with David Wallace about a possible promotion.

73
00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.920
Dwight and Toby join forces to investigate whether Daryl submitted a fraudulent medical claim.

74
00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.780
And Pam works to lock down her RSVPs for her and Jim's wedding.

75
00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:06.980
I found that storyline so relatable.

76
00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.300
Will you just RSVP, people?

77
00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:10.720
Just RSVP!

78
00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:13.620
When Meredith says, I'll just text you the day of the wedding.

79
00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:17.240
I mean, did you get texts on the day of your wedding?

80
00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:18.140
Because I did.

81
00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:18.560
Yeah.

82
00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:25.960
I was like, you may not be my friend anymore, person who texted me on my wedding morning to ask me about, like, parking instructions.

83
00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:30.040
I got a text the morning of my wedding about, would this outfit be appropriate?

84
00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:31.800
What's the attire again?

85
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.340
Is it real fancy or could I wear something casual?

86
00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:35.520
See, now listen.

87
00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:40.060
Ange, if you text me the morning of my wedding to ask me a question, that's fine.

88
00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:40.780
But you know what?

89
00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:41.280
I'm glad I wouldn't.

90
00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:43.560
That's the difference.

91
00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:45.740
BFFs get a pass.

92
00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:48.520
BFFs do get a pass, but they also know better.

93
00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:49.360
Yeah.

94
00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:51.420
Text another wedding gueSt.

95
00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:52.860
You know someone who's going.

96
00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:57.520
Text your most reliable friend who saved the invitation.

97
00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.160
Text the friend you know in the wedding party.

98
00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:00.680
Yeah.

99
00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:01.940
That's their job.

100
00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:03.680
Leave the bride and groom alone.

101
00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:04.260
Okay.

102
00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:05.940
We could really go off on that.

103
00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:07.460
I mean, listen.

104
00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:10.000
Clearly, it meant so much to us.

105
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.700
We moved it up to the top of the show.

106
00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:12.000
Yeah.

107
00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:13.640
We didn't even wait to get to that scene.

108
00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:14.040
I know.

109
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:15.620
All right.

110
00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.080
Let's do some fast facts.

111
00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:18.840
Fast fact number one.

112
00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:25.060
This episode is going to kick off a major story arc for Jim, in which he becomes the

113
00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:27.420
co-manager of the Scranton branch.

114
00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.380
Now, this is also going to signal a wardrobe change for Jim.

115
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:32.140
Did you notice?

116
00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.520
Boo-ba-doop-boo, wardrobe change.

117
00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:39.460
Jim wears a jacket instead of his usual shirt and tie.

118
00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:41.180
Not just any jacket, a suit.

119
00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:41.940
Yeah.

120
00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.040
This is going to continue while he is co-manager.

121
00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:47.820
I guess jacket means you take things seriously.

122
00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.640
Look at Pam when she'd become a salesperson.

123
00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:51.980
They're the jacket couple now.

124
00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:52.680
We are.

125
00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:54.200
We're so cute.

126
00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:57.500
You know how Dwight wore his long sleeves for Charles Minor?

127
00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:58.140
Yeah.

128
00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:04.860
These are ways that our wardrobe department can really, you know, signal storytelling.

129
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.620
I think it's very cool, and I wanted to give them a shout out.

130
00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:08.380
Love it.

131
00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:09.520
Loved our wardrobe department.

132
00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:10.940
I loved that fast fact.

133
00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:12.360
Thank you, Ange.

134
00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:14.540
Fast fact number two.

135
00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.600
This episode is a very interesting pairing as part of its B story.

136
00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:24.480
This feels like one of those assignments that we've heard about, the taking of the two

137
00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.660
people, Dwight and Toby.

138
00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.420
When have we had a Dwight-Toby huge storyline?

139
00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:30.880
I can't think of one.

140
00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:31.640
I thought the same thing.

141
00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:35.000
They were on a card, and they got sent off to the writer's pod.

142
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:41.100
Now, this B story was also ranked number seven on Screen Rant's Best B Plots.

143
00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:42.980
Do you know what was number one?

144
00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:43.860
No.

145
00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:45.620
Dwight's Concussion.

146
00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:47.440
Aw, with Pam.

147
00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:47.920
Yeah.

148
00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:49.240
I did love that one.

149
00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:56.860
So, incidentally, while Paul Lieberstein was starring in this very popular B story, he

150
00:05:56.860 --> 00:06:00.640
was also our sole showrunner for this season, season six.

151
00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:05.920
In season five, he shared that title with Jen Salata, but at this point, Jen was starting

152
00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:09.940
to develop some other projects, so she took a step back in season six.

153
00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:11.780
She was the consulting producer.

154
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.300
She worked three days a week.

155
00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:18.140
So, Paul, our showrunner, was starring in this episode.

156
00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:19.000
I don't know.

157
00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.480
It just amazes me how he juggled it all.

158
00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:23.880
I mean, I feel like he was living there for a bit.

159
00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:25.740
Do you think he ever slept there?

160
00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:27.640
I feel like maybe at least once.

161
00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:28.420
We should ask him.

162
00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:29.060
We should ask.

163
00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:33.020
Now, fast fact number three, Angela, I am handing over to you.

164
00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:34.440
What would you like to tell us?

165
00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:38.000
I would like to talk about the deleted scenes for this episode.

166
00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.960
I am titling this fast fact because I guess I titled my fast facts.

167
00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:42.500
Oh.

168
00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:44.240
I'm titling my fast fact.

169
00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:46.020
Stay with me.

170
00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:48.000
Long, long, long, long.

171
00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:49.260
Oh.

172
00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:51.460
Long, long, long, long.

173
00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.540
Should I be titling my fast facts?

174
00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:55.540
No.

175
00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:58.140
I think we're all fine not to.

176
00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:02.460
You guys, this episode was a very long script.

177
00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:03.120
Oh.

178
00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.920
And a lot of our scripts came in very long, but I just thought this one really highlights

179
00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:12.480
how much didn't make it in that was written on the page, and I wanted to share that with you.

180
00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:13.040
Okay.

181
00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:17.540
It's a little insight, you guys, into the deleted scenes of an episode of The Office.

182
00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:23.860
So I was trading emails with Randy Cordray, and he said that there was so much material for our episodes

183
00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:28.720
that our editor's first cuts were usually about 45 minutes long.

184
00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:33.820
And we had to narrow that down to 21 minutes and 30 seconds to broadcast the show.

185
00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:36.540
So they would cut more than half.

186
00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:37.400
Exactly.

187
00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:39.160
That brings me to this script.

188
00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:40.080
Jenna, I looked.

189
00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:42.160
The shooting draft was 41 pages.

190
00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:45.300
Okay, so it's usually a page equals a minute.

191
00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:46.740
We've talked about that before.

192
00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:47.220
Right.

193
00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:50.220
So they were going to have to edit out more than half the script.

194
00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:55.920
I went to the shooting draft, and I read the shooting draft as I watched the episode that

195
00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:03.100
was broadcast, and there were six talking heads that never made it in, eight full scenes

196
00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:09.160
that never made it, and then five scenes were actually extended and longer than what made

197
00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:09.960
it into the screen.

198
00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:15.520
And I was trading emails with Randy, and he brought up a great, great note, which is the

199
00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:20.600
whole Dwight and Toby runner, as well as some of the Michael and Andy runner, really had to

200
00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:21.640
be cut for this episode.

201
00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:25.400
And there are elements that didn't make it in that would have really kind of explained

202
00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:26.860
a little bit more what was happening.

203
00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:27.480
Mm-hmm.

204
00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:29.440
And I'm going to share some of those as we go.

205
00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:30.340
Wow.

206
00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:34.200
This episode was almost like two A stories to me.

207
00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:35.420
Yes, exactly.

208
00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:36.000
Exactly.

209
00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:41.740
But if you think about it, I mean, six talking heads, eight whole scenes.

210
00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:42.900
Eight whole scenes.

211
00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:43.680
Wow.

212
00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:44.140
Right?

213
00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:44.820
Yeah.

214
00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:46.720
But you're going to give us the dish.

215
00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:47.660
I'm going to give you the dish.

216
00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:48.920
You're going to spill the tea.

217
00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:49.940
Is that what it is?

218
00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:50.560
Spill the tea?

219
00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:51.520
Do you spill the tea?

220
00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:56.540
I think you spill the tea, and you serve the dish, and you give the scoop.

221
00:08:56.780 --> 00:08:57.340
What else?

222
00:08:57.460 --> 00:08:58.200
What's another one?

223
00:08:58.380 --> 00:08:59.100
I don't know.

224
00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:00.440
That's my fast fact.

225
00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:05.840
That just shows you what we had to do every week to trim it to network broadcaSt.

226
00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:09.220
I enjoyed this top of show.

227
00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:11.080
Should we go to a break?

228
00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:16.940
And when we come back, we will spill the tea and give the dish and tell the things.

229
00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:18.920
We'll tell all the things.

230
00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:19.600
All right.

231
00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:30.500
All right.

232
00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:33.740
This episode starts with a cold open between Michael and Oscar.

233
00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:38.580
Michael has called Oscar into his office for advice on an upcoming medical procedure.

234
00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:41.360
And Oscar is concerned.

235
00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:42.860
He's like, Michael, are you okay?

236
00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:43.540
What's going on?

237
00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:49.160
Michael reveals it's a colonoscopy and wonders if, in Oscar's experience, there's anything

238
00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:52.440
he can do to make it more pleasurable for himself or the doctor.

239
00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:56.860
And Oscar, of course, walks out of Michael's office.

240
00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:57.740
Yes.

241
00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:01.040
Now, I always love scenes between Steve and Oscar.

242
00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:02.980
They are so good together.

243
00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:04.460
They're a good comedy duo.

244
00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:06.420
This scene made me cringe.

245
00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:06.940
Yeah.

246
00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:10.300
But Oscar's performance is so good.

247
00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:11.760
Oh, my God.

248
00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:12.520
Oscar's amazing.

249
00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:13.860
I caught an improv moment.

250
00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:14.440
Oh.

251
00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:15.120
Yeah.

252
00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:16.420
So it's not in the script.

253
00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:17.540
I went to the shooting draft.

254
00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:21.960
There's this moment where Oscar says, what's the procedure, if you don't mind me asking?

255
00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:24.840
And he scooches his chair real close to Michael's desk.

256
00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:30.920
Oscar Nunez came up with that sort of physical improv in the moment to sort of, you know, try

257
00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:32.080
to bond with Michael, right?

258
00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:33.260
What's happening, Michael?

259
00:10:33.260 --> 00:10:35.240
I thought that was just smart acting.

260
00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:41.600
Well, Steve and Oscar have a very similar improvisation style, and it's based on small

261
00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:45.440
details and slow timing, like-

262
00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:46.180
Patience.

263
00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:47.040
